[ me ]

| (2)  Our 500S will have 256Mb of memory.  Any guidelines on how much
| swap space is appropriate?  (Yeah, yeah, I know there isn't a general
| answer.  But I'll ask anyway.)  I was thinking of 128Mb on the RZ26,
| and 128Mb on each of the Fuji drives.  Seem reasonable?

[ Jaye Mathisen ]

| Depends entirely on your workload, I don't see how anybody could answer
| this with any degree of accuracy.

Yes, I *know* it's essentially unanswerable.  (Isn't that what I implied
above?)  But in general, one can usually get within a factor of two (or
thereabouts) of a sensible value.  Generally, there's not much point in
having a swapspace:RAM ratio of 10:1, since if you really need that much
swap, things will probably thrash horribly.  (Again, there are numerous
exceptions:  Maybe a single large process has such good locality (both
instruction and data) that paging will be near a theoretical minimum.
Or maybe one would want to run the program even in spite of a horrible
thrash situation, it being the only alternative to not running the job
at all.)

But ...  In general, one can at least come up with vague guidelines, in
part based on how the operating system handles swap allocation.  Example:
In ancient times (and even today, on many UNIXes) the kernel allocated
swap space for a new process (certainly after an exec(); perhaps not on
a fork(), if the vfork() model is used) before the process could run.
But other UNIXes (e.g. SGI's IRIX) don't necessarily allocate swap space
for a process, unless it's actually required.  (This has the side-effect
that some process may unexpectedly have to be killed off part way through
execution, if it needs to be swapped out but there's no space for it.  So
SGI provides the option of configuring a kernel to use the "traditional"
method of swap allocation.)

The whole point of this long digression is basically just a prelude to a
re-phrasing of my original question:  Which method of swap allocation is
used in OSF/1?  The traditional "don't run this process unless you can
allocate swap space for it first" method?  Or the "don't allocate swap
space for this process unless you need it, and kill it if you fail" one?
The answer to that question will certainly be relevant to the question of
how much swap space to configure.

[ me ]

| (3)  I don't have a spec sheet for the RZ26.  Does anyone know how it
| compares, performance-wise, with the Fuji M2694S?

[ Jaye Mathisen ]

| The RZ26 is 1.05GB 3.5" drive, 10.5ms access times.  

Thanks for the information, but which "access time" are you referring to?
The head positioning time (and, if so, is it track-to-track, maximum, or
average), or the average rotational latency?  I suspect head positioning
(and probably average rather than single-track or maximum), partly since
a 10.5ms average latency would imply a rotational speed less than 3600rpm.

If my guess is correct, what about the other important figure:  How fast do
these thing spin?  (The Fuji M2694S is 5400rpm, the fastest I've seen in a
reasonably low-cost drive.)

